## Explanation regarding user data handling

The method retrieves the `data` field from the container node (e.g., `user`). It then applies an early-return check: if `data` is missing (`null`) or is not an object node, the method exits immediately without performing any normalization or merging.

This matters because a `user.data` field can arrive as an array rather than an object. Since an array node returns false for `isObject()`, the guard causes the method to skip processing entirely — the array-typed `data` is left untouched, and no merge into `ext.data` occurs. Only object-typed `data` proceeds to be merged with existing `ext.data` (via `jsonMerger.merge`) or copied into `ext` when no ext data exists.

Note that this method handles the `data`/`ext.data` relationship specifically; other user fields like `keywords` (array-to-comma-separated) and `gender` (array-to-first-element) are normalized separately in the `normalizeNode` logic, not here.

